{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["זרחה — here: HaShem. And the other: והשמש זרחה:" "<b>על המים וגו' אדומים כדם</b> — sun shone and departed. And the tzara'at shone on his forehead. That is, if the sun shone and already departed, so he came and went while still night, then he has no blood-money. But if the sun shone upon him — that he came by day — just as the sun shone upon the waters [as] blood, he has blood. And so it is written there too: אדומים כדם — that if he waited until the sun shone upon him, he has blood. And one [more]: וזרחה לכם יראי שמי — for the righteous, who are healed by it, as written: ומרפא בכנפיה. But the wicked — it strikes and consumes them, as stated: הנה יום בא בוער כתנור וגו' ולהט אותם וגו'. And therefore זרחה לו — the tzara'at shone on his forehead."], :book :exodus, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {1 "The comment weaves together four occurrences of the root זרח across Scripture: Exodus 22:2 (זרחה השמש), the token of blood on the waters in some context (על המים ... אדומים כדם), Malachi 3:20 (וזרחה לכם יראי שמי), and the tzara'at of the metzora. The phrase אדומים כדם and the reference to waters likely allude to 2 Kings 3:22, where water appears אדומים כדם."}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-12T19:14:04.220246330Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}